This one was built for my family's jewelry business in Cambodia, and their invoices needed two things most off-the-shelf software doesn't do: output in Khmer and English on the same document, and pricing that depends on the day's gold market price instead of a fixed catalog price. I built this to handle both directly rather than work around a tool that assumes neither.

## Gold pricing, not generic invoicing

Each item's price comes from its weight, its gold purity, and whatever the market price is that day — weight times gold-mix percentage times market price, plus a separate labor charge per piece. Purity isn't fixed either: the shop prices in 75%, 80%, 90%, and 100% gold, set per item. One piece of business logic I just had to encode as a literal rule rather than derive: the shop rounds a total up past a 0.7 decimal threshold and down below it, not the usual round-to-nearest-0.5, so the code has an explicit `>= 0.7` check instead of a general rounding function.

## Two languages, one document

The bilingual part isn't a language switch in the UI — the invoice itself is bilingual, every label printed in Khmer with the English directly alongside it on the same line, because that's the document that goes to the customer. The editing screens work the same way: Khmer first, English second, on every field in the app, not just the printed output. The printed invoice also carries a QR code for ABA Pay or Wing, the two payment apps customers there actually use.

## Why a desktop app

This runs as an Electron app rather than a web app because everything about it is local. It saves finished invoices straight to a folder the shop picks once in Settings, organizes them into a subfolder per client automatically, and writes both an HTML copy (for the built-in preview) and a PDF (for printing) for each one — none of that needs a server. The same offline-first goal reaches down to the framework itself: there's no bundler or build step, React and Babel ship as local files in the repo, and every component compiles from JSX to JS live in the browser through a `type="text/babel"` script tag instead of a webpack or Vite pipeline.

