## What was hard

Most of the hard parts weren't the invoicing logic, they were Electron itself. Getting the built-in preview to load a saved HTML file inside an iframe meant turning off `webSecurity` in the main process — acceptable for a single-purpose offline tool, not something I'd want near a browser context. Packaging it as a Windows installer surfaced an actual bug in electron-builder's own file-writing step: an intermittent `EBUSY` error from Windows still holding the previous build's executable locked. Rather than wait on an upstream fix, I patched the dependency directly with a small postinstall script that retries the write with backoff instead.

<Callout label="Limitation">

There's no real database behind any of this — saved invoices are just HTML and PDF files in per-client folders, and the saved-files browser gets each invoice's total by regex-matching the pattern back out of its own saved HTML rather than reading it from anywhere structured. It works because one shop's invoice volume is small, but it's the kind of shortcut that wouldn't hold up if more than one till needed to see the same data.

</Callout>
